WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.600
Question 0003: what does this code do?
A student-level question.

2
00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:10.000
One part: read a short piece of code,
run it on an example, and explain what it does.

3
00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:15.833
A short function takes a list of numbers.
What does it do to the list?

4
00:00:16.633 --> 00:00:20.933
Here is the question's code, in Python.
Let's work out what it does.

5
00:00:21.633 --> 00:00:26.867
The function mystery takes a list A
of integers and changes it in place.

6
00:00:26.867 --> 00:00:29.267
First n, the number of elements in A.

7
00:00:29.267 --> 00:00:33.667
The outer loop: i runs over
every index, from 0 to n − 1.

8
00:00:33.667 --> 00:00:38.067
The inner loop: j also runs over all of them,
from 0, not only over those after i.

9
00:00:38.067 --> 00:00:41.167
If A[i] is less than A[j],
the two are swapped.

10
00:00:41.467 --> 00:00:45.467
At first sight it looks like a descending sort:
a small element at i swaps with a larger one.

11
00:00:45.467 --> 00:00:49.467
But before answering, run a small example.
The question asks for an array of size 4.

12
00:00:50.067 --> 00:00:54.800
The page's box runs the code
on the page's array: 3, 1, 4, 2.

13
00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:59.033
Comparison 1: i = 0 and j = 0.
Is 3 < 3? No, no swap.

14
00:00:59.033 --> 00:01:02.167
Comparison 2: j = 1.
Is 3 < 1? No.

15
00:01:02.167 --> 00:01:07.133
Comparison 3: j = 2. Is 3 < 4? Yes.
Swap, and A is 4, 1, 3, 2.

16
00:01:07.133 --> 00:01:12.100
Comparison 4: is 4 < 2? No.
At the end of i = 0, the maximum 4 sits in A[0].

17
00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:17.233
The "until the end of i" button completes i = 1:
one swap, at j = 0, of 1 with 4.

18
00:01:17.233 --> 00:01:22.333
End of i = 2: one swap, at j = 1.
The 3 goes in before the 4: 1, 3, 4, 2.

19
00:01:22.333 --> 00:01:27.133
End of i = 3: two swaps, at j = 1 and j = 2.
The array is 1, 2, 3, 4.

20
00:01:27.133 --> 00:01:31.133
The answer: the code sorts in ascending order,
smallest to largest. Not descending!

21
00:01:31.933 --> 00:01:37.033
Back to the code: run it on the example
beside the box, stopping at every swap.

22
00:01:37.033 --> 00:01:40.033
At the start n = 4,
and A is 3, 1, 4, 2.

23
00:01:40.033 --> 00:01:45.567
Comparison 1: i = 0, j = 0.
3 < 3 does not hold.

24
00:01:45.567 --> 00:01:50.700
Comparison 2: 3 < 1. No.

25
00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:56.233
Comparison 3: 3 < 4. Yes,
and the swap brings 4 to A[0].

26
00:01:56.233 --> 00:02:01.767
Comparison 4: 4 < 2. No. The inner loop
is over, and the maximum is in A[0].

27
00:02:01.767 --> 00:02:07.300
At i = 1 the very first comparison swaps:
1 < 4, and the 1 moves to the front.

28
00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:12.767
Now A[1] is 4, the maximum,
so the rest of the j's of i = 1 swap nothing.

29
00:02:12.767 --> 00:02:18.300
At i = 2: 3 < 1 no, but 3 < 4 yes.
The 3 goes in before the 4: 1, 3, 4, 2.

30
00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:23.767
At j = 2 and j = 3, A[2] is 4,
the maximum, and nothing swaps.

31
00:02:23.767 --> 00:02:29.300
At i = 3: 2 < 3, and the 2 swaps with the 3.
The array is 1, 2, 4, 3.

32
00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:34.833
At j = 2: 3 < 4, one more swap,
and the array is 1, 2, 3, 4.

33
00:02:34.833 --> 00:02:40.433
The last comparison, 4 < 4, does not hold.
16 comparisons, 5 swaps, an ascending sort.

34
00:02:41.233 --> 00:02:45.700
Why does it always work? Look at the array
at the end of every iteration of i.

35
00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:51.200
The claim: at the end of iteration i, A[0..i]
is sorted, and A[i] is the maximum of the array.

36
00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:56.700
Step 1, i = 0: every larger element swaps
with A[0], so at the end A[0] is the maximum.

37
00:02:56.700 --> 00:03:02.000
Step 2, j < i: A[i] goes into its place
in the sorted part, as in insertion sort.

38
00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:07.033
The larger ones move one place to the right,
and the maximum lands exactly in A[i].

39
00:03:07.033 --> 00:03:12.533
Step 3, j ≥ i: A[i] is already the maximum,
so the condition never holds and nothing swaps.

40
00:03:12.533 --> 00:03:17.433
Step 4: after i = n − 1, the sorted part
is the whole array. An ascending sort.

41
00:03:18.233 --> 00:03:23.200
The cost: always n² comparisons, even
when the array is already sorted. Time Θ(n²).

42
00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:27.200
No early exit, and the sort is in place:
O(1) extra memory.

43
00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:31.200
On 4 elements, 16 comparisons.
On 1,000 elements, a million comparisons.

44
00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:37.500
The trap: guessing the direction from the condition.
"Descending" is right only if j starts at i + 1.

45
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:42.667
That is selection sort, and the result is 4, 3, 2, 1.
The same condition, the opposite direction.

46
00:03:42.667 --> 00:03:46.667
There each i brings the maximum of A[i..n − 1]
to A[i], so the large ones come first.

47
00:03:47.465 --> 00:03:51.465
In short: the code sorts in ascending order,
always with n² comparisons.

48
00:03:51.465 --> 00:03:56.365
Try an array of your own in the box
on the page of question 0003 on the site.
